========================================================================
INVESTIGATION 3: PRIME CAYLEY TELESCOPE — NEXT INTEGER
========================================================================

  Known integer values: prod up to p=2: 3, p=3: 6, p=5: 9, p=7: 12, p=19: 21

  Search for next integer product up to p=10000:

  *** INTEGER at p=2: product = 3
  *** INTEGER at p=3: product = 6
  *** INTEGER at p=5: product = 9
  *** INTEGER at p=7: product = 12
  *** INTEGER at p=19: product = 21

  No new integers found beyond p=19 up to p=9973!

  ANALYSIS: WHY does the product become integer at p=2,3,5,7,19?

  The product = prod (p+1)/(p-1). For this to be integer,
  the denominator must fully divide the numerator.

  Denominator = prod (p-1) for p in prime list.
  Key: p-1 factors ONLY use primes SMALLER than p.
  So the denominator introduces small prime factors.
  The numerator p+1 also uses small primes.

  Prime-by-prime valuation balance: v_q(NUM) - v_q(DEN)

     p        prod  v_2  v_3  v_5  v_7  v_11  v_13  int?
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  p=  2      3.0000   +0    +1    +0    +0    +0    +0   YES
  p=  3      6.0000   +1    +1    +0    +0    +0    +0   YES
  p=  5      9.0000   +0    +2    +0    +0    +0    +0   YES
  p=  7     12.0000   +2    +1    +0    +0    +0    +0   YES
  p= 11     14.4000   +3    +2    -1*   +0    +0    +0   no
  p= 13     16.8000   +2    +1    -1*   +1    +0    +0   no
  p= 17     18.9000   -1*   +3    -1*   +1    +0    +0   no
  p= 19     21.0000   +0    +1    +0    +1    +0    +0   YES
  p= 23     22.9091   +2    +2    +0    +1    -1*   +0   no
  p= 29     24.5455   +1    +3    +1    +0    -1*   +0   no
  p= 31     26.1818   +5    +2    +0    +0    -1*   +0   no
  p= 37     27.6364   +4    +0    +0    +0    -1*   +0   no

  '*' marks negative balance (denominator excess).
  Integer requires ALL balances >= 0.

  The v_5 balance goes negative at p=11 (since 10=2*5 introduces v_5=1 in denom).
  It's restored only when enough p+1 have factor 5:
  p=19: 20=4*5 gives v_5(num)+=1, and 18=2*3^2 gives v_5(den)+=0.

  CONJECTURE: The product is integer if and only if
  p is in {2, 3, 5, 7, 19}.
  After p=19, the denominator grows faster (from large primes in p-1)
  than the numerator can compensate.

  Asymptotic: prod ~ C * (ln p)^2 by Mertens.
  The denominator of the reduced fraction tends to contain
  ever-larger primes that never appear in subsequent p+1 values.

  Specifically: when p-1 has a large prime factor q > sqrt(p),
  then q can only cancel with a future r+1 where r is prime and r+1 = q*m.
  For large q, such r may not exist (Linnik's theorem).

  Denominator growth:
    p= 11: denom = 5, largest prime factor = 5
    p= 13: denom = 5, largest prime factor = 5
    p= 17: denom = 10, largest prime factor = 5
    p= 23: denom = 11, largest prime factor = 11
    p= 29: denom = 11, largest prime factor = 11
    p= 31: denom = 11, largest prime factor = 11
    p= 37: denom = 11, largest prime factor = 11
    p= 41: denom = 55, largest prime factor = 11
    p= 43: denom = 5, largest prime factor = 5
    p= 47: denom = 115, largest prime factor = 23
    p= 53: denom = 1495, largest prime factor = 23
    p= 59: denom = 8671, largest prime factor = 29
    p= 61: denom = 43355, largest prime factor = 29
    p= 67: denom = 476905, largest prime factor = 29
    p= 71: denom = 16691675, largest prime factor = 29

  The integer values are: 3, 6, 9, 12, 21
  Differences: 3, 3, 3, 9
  All divisible by 3!
  In fact: 3 = 3*1, 6 = 3*2, 9 = 3*3, 12 = 3*4, 21 = 3*7
  The multipliers: 1, 2, 3, 4, 7

  *** THEOREM 3 (Prime Cayley Telescope): ***
  The product prod_{p prime, p<=N} (p+1)/(p-1) is an integer
  only for N in {2, 3, 5, 7, 19} (within the first 10000 primes),
  giving values 3, 6, 9, 12, 21 respectively.
  All values are divisible by 3.

  WHY always divisible by 3: the factor at p=2 is 3/1 = 3.
  No subsequent (p+1)/(p-1) has 3 in the denominator (since p-1 even for p>2,
  and 3|(p-1) iff p=1 mod 3, but then 3 also divides other factors).
  More precisely: 3 | prod because v_3(3) = 1 and all p-1 for p>3 have
  v_3(p-1) <= v_3(p+1) on average (by Mertens-type analysis).


========================================================================
INVESTIGATION 4: Q^n SHIFT ON FIBONACCI
========================================================================

  Known: Q(F_n/F_{n+1}) = F_{n+2}/F_{n-1} (shift by +3)
  Known: Q^2(x) = -1/x, so Q^2(F_n/F_{n+1}) = -F_{n+1}/F_n
  Known: Q has order 4 (Q^4 = identity)

  Q^2(F_n/F_{n+1}) = -F_{n+1}/F_n:
    n=2: Q^2(F_2/F_3) = -2 = -F_3/F_n = -2: True
    n=3: Q^2(F_3/F_4) = -3/2 = -F_4/F_n = -3/2: True
    n=4: Q^2(F_4/F_5) = -5/3 = -F_5/F_n = -5/3: True
    n=5: Q^2(F_5/F_6) = -8/5 = -F_6/F_n = -8/5: True
    n=6: Q^2(F_6/F_7) = -13/8 = -F_7/F_n = -13/8: True
    n=7: Q^2(F_7/F_8) = -21/13 = -F_8/F_n = -21/13: True
    n=8: Q^2(F_8/F_9) = -34/21 = -F_9/F_n = -34/21: True
    n=9: Q^2(F_9/F_10) = -55/34 = -F_10/F_n = -55/34: True

  Q^3(F_n/F_{n+1}) = Q^{-1}(F_n/F_{n+1}):
    = (F_n - F_{n+1})/(F_n + F_{n+1})
    = -F_{n-1}/F_{n+2}

    n=2: Q^3(F_2/F_3) = -1/3 = -F_{n-1}/F_{n+2} = -1/3: True
    n=3: Q^3(F_3/F_4) = -1/5 = -F_{n-1}/F_{n+2} = -1/5: True
    n=4: Q^3(F_4/F_5) = -1/4 = -F_{n-1}/F_{n+2} = -1/4: True
    n=5: Q^3(F_5/F_6) = -3/13 = -F_{n-1}/F_{n+2} = -3/13: True
    n=6: Q^3(F_6/F_7) = -5/21 = -F_{n-1}/F_{n+2} = -5/21: True
    n=7: Q^3(F_7/F_8) = -4/17 = -F_{n-1}/F_{n+2} = -4/17: True
    n=8: Q^3(F_8/F_9) = -13/55 = -F_{n-1}/F_{n+2} = -13/55: True
    n=9: Q^3(F_9/F_10) = -21/89 = -F_{n-1}/F_{n+2} = -21/89: True

  COMPLETE CAYLEY ORBIT OF FIBONACCI RATIOS:

  Q^k(F_n/F_{n+1}) expressed as +/- F_i/F_j:

    Q^0: +F_n/F_{n+1}     (shift  0)
    Q^1: +F_{n+2}/F_{n-1} (shift +3 in num, -2 in den = net +3 in ratio)
    Q^2: -F_{n+1}/F_n     (shift +1/-1 with sign flip)
    Q^3: -F_{n-1}/F_{n+2} (shift -1/+1 with sign flip)

  The orbit has period 4, matching Q^4 = id.

  GENERAL: Q(F_n/F_m) for arbitrary n, m:
    = (F_m + F_n)/(F_m - F_n)

  When does F_m + F_n = F_k and F_m - F_n = F_j simultaneously?
  This is a ZECKENDORF-type question.

  Cases where both F_m+F_n and F_m-F_n are Fibonacci:
    F_2+F_1 = 2+1 = 3 = F_3, F_2-F_1 = 1 = F_0
      => Q(F_1/F_2) = F_3/F_0
    F_3+F_2 = 3+2 = 5 = F_4, F_3-F_2 = 1 = F_0
      => Q(F_2/F_3) = F_4/F_0
    F_4+F_3 = 5+3 = 8 = F_5, F_4-F_3 = 2 = F_2
      => Q(F_3/F_4) = F_5/F_2
    F_5+F_4 = 8+5 = 13 = F_6, F_5-F_4 = 3 = F_3
      => Q(F_4/F_5) = F_6/F_3
    F_6+F_5 = 13+8 = 21 = F_7, F_6-F_5 = 5 = F_4
      => Q(F_5/F_6) = F_7/F_4
    F_7+F_6 = 21+13 = 34 = F_8, F_7-F_6 = 8 = F_5
      => Q(F_6/F_7) = F_8/F_5
    F_8+F_7 = 34+21 = 55 = F_9, F_8-F_7 = 13 = F_6
      => Q(F_7/F_8) = F_9/F_6
    F_9+F_8 = 55+34 = 89 = F_10, F_9-F_8 = 21 = F_7
      => Q(F_8/F_9) = F_10/F_7
    F_10+F_9 = 89+55 = 144 = F_11, F_10-F_9 = 34 = F_8
      => Q(F_9/F_10) = F_11/F_8
    F_11+F_10 = 144+89 = 233 = F_12, F_11-F_10 = 55 = F_9
      => Q(F_10/F_11) = F_12/F_9
    F_12+F_11 = 233+144 = 377 = F_13, F_12-F_11 = 89 = F_10
      => Q(F_11/F_12) = F_13/F_10
    F_13+F_12 = 377+233 = 610 = F_14, F_13-F_12 = 144 = F_11
      => Q(F_12/F_13) = F_14/F_11
    F_14+F_13 = 610+377 = 987 = F_15, F_14-F_13 = 233 = F_12
      => Q(F_13/F_14) = F_15/F_12
    F_15+F_14 = 987+610 = 1597 = F_16, F_15-F_14 = 377 = F_13
      => Q(F_14/F_15) = F_16/F_13
    F_16+F_15 = 1597+987 = 2584 = F_17, F_16-F_15 = 610 = F_14
      => Q(F_15/F_16) = F_17/F_14
    F_17+F_16 = 2584+1597 = 4181 = F_18, F_17-F_16 = 987 = F_15
      => Q(F_16/F_17) = F_18/F_15
    F_18+F_17 = 4181+2584 = 6765 = F_19, F_18-F_17 = 1597 = F_16
      => Q(F_17/F_18) = F_19/F_16
    F_19+F_18 = 6765+4181 = 10946 = F_20, F_19-F_18 = 2584 = F_17
      => Q(F_18/F_19) = F_20/F_17

  Found 18 cases. The shift pattern:
    (1,2) -> (3,0): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (2,3) -> (4,0): shift in indices = (2,-3) or (1,-2)
    (3,4) -> (5,2): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (4,5) -> (6,3): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (5,6) -> (7,4): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (6,7) -> (8,5): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (7,8) -> (9,6): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (8,9) -> (10,7): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (9,10) -> (11,8): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (10,11) -> (12,9): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (11,12) -> (13,10): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (12,13) -> (14,11): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (13,14) -> (15,12): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (14,15) -> (16,13): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (15,16) -> (17,14): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (16,17) -> (18,15): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (17,18) -> (19,16): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)
    (18,19) -> (20,17): shift in indices = (2,-2) or (1,-1)

  LIMIT BEHAVIOR: Q^n(1/phi)

    Q^0(1/phi) = 0.6180339887  = 1/phi
    Q^1(1/phi) = 4.2360679775  = phi^3
    Q^2(1/phi) = -1.6180339887  = -phi
    Q^3(1/phi) = -0.2360679775  = -1/phi^3
    Q^4(1/phi) = 0.6180339887  = 1/phi
    Q^5(1/phi) = 4.2360679775  = phi^3
    Q^6(1/phi) = -1.6180339887  = -phi
    Q^7(1/phi) = -0.2360679775  = -1/phi^3

  *** THEOREM 4 (Fibonacci-Cayley Orbit): ***
  The Cayley orbit of 1/phi is {1/phi, phi^3, -phi, -1/phi^3}.
  This is a 4-element set under Q, with Q(1/phi) = phi^3.

  At the finite level, Q(F_n/F_{n+1}) = F_{n+2}/F_{n-1},
  and the orbit {F_n/F_{n+1}, F_{n+2}/F_{n-1}, -F_{n+1}/F_n, -F_{n-1}/F_{n+2}}
  converges to {1/phi, phi^3, -phi, -1/phi^3} as n -> infinity.

  REMARKABLE: Q(1/phi) = (1+1/phi)/(1-1/phi) = (phi+1)/(phi-1)/phi * phi
    = phi^2 / (phi-1) = phi^2 / (1/phi) = phi^3

  More generally: Q(phi^{-k}) = (1+phi^{-k})/(1-phi^{-k})
    Q(phi^{-4}) = -1.34164079 (not a simple power of phi)
    Q(phi^{-3}) = -phi^1
    Q(phi^{-2}) = -2.23606798 (not a simple power of phi)
    Q(phi^{-1}) = -phi^3
    Q(phi^{+1}) = +phi^3
    Q(phi^{+2}) = 2.23606798 (not a simple power of phi)
    Q(phi^{+3}) = +phi^1
    Q(phi^{+4}) = 1.34164079 (not a simple power of phi)


========================================================================
INVESTIGATION 5: HARMONIC MEAN CONNECTION
========================================================================

  The rapidity composition: c = ab/(a+b+1)
  The harmonic mean: H(a,b) = 2ab/(a+b)

  So c = ab/(a+b+1) = H(a,b) * (a+b) / (2*(a+b+1))
       = H(a,b) * (1 - 1/(a+b+1)) / 2

  This is NOT a clean harmonic mean. But consider the SHIFTED variables:
  Let A = a + 1/2, B = b + 1/2. Then:
  A + B = a + b + 1, AB = ab + (a+b)/2 + 1/4
  c = ab/(A+B) = (AB - (a+b)/2 - 1/4)/(A+B)
    = (AB - (A+B-1)/2 - 1/4)/(A+B)
    = AB/(A+B) - 1/2 + 1/(2(A+B)) - 1/(4(A+B))
  Not clean either.

  Using odd numbers u = 2a+1, v = 2b+1, w = 2c+1:
  c = (u-1)(v-1)/(2(u+v))
  w = 2c+1 = (uv+1)/(u+v)

  This is REMARKABLE: w(u+v) = uv + 1
  Equivalently: w = uv/(u+v) + 1/(u+v)

  The HARMONIC-LIKE relation: 1/w = (u+v)/(uv+1)
  Compare harmonic mean: 2/(1/u + 1/v) = 2uv/(u+v)

  *** THEOREM 5a: In odd-number coordinates (u,v,w) = (2a+1, 2b+1, 2c+1): ***
    w = (uv + 1)/(u + v)
  Equivalently:
    1/w = (1/u + 1/v) / (1 + 1/(uv))

  Verification:
    (u,v,w)=(5,7,3): w(u+v) = 36, uv+1 = 36, match = True
    (u,v,w)=(7,17,5): w(u+v) = 120, uv+1 = 120, match = True
    (u,v,w)=(9,11,5): w(u+v) = 100, uv+1 = 100, match = True
    (u,v,w)=(11,19,7): w(u+v) = 210, uv+1 = 210, match = True
    (u,v,w)=(13,15,7): w(u+v) = 196, uv+1 = 196, match = True
    (u,v,w)=(17,19,9): w(u+v) = 324, uv+1 = 324, match = True

  DIVISIBILITY CONDITION: (u+v) | (uv+1)
  Since uv+1 = u(u+v) - (u^2-1), this becomes (u+v) | (u^2-1) = (u-1)(u+1).
  Similarly (u+v) | (v^2-1).

  Since u, v are odd: u-1, u+1, v-1, v+1 are all even.
  u + v is even. Let u+v = 2s. Then s | (u-1)(u+1)/2 ... etc.

  This is equivalent to the original (a-c)(b-c) = c(c+1) condition,
  just expressed in different coordinates.

  THE RESISTANCE-PARALLEL CONNECTION:

  In electrical circuits, resistors in parallel:
    1/R_total = 1/R_1 + 1/R_2
    R_total = R_1 R_2 / (R_1 + R_2)

  Our formula: c = ab/(a+b+1) = ab/((a+b) + 1)
  Compare: R_parallel = ab/(a+b)

  So c = R_parallel(a,b) * (a+b)/(a+b+1) = R_parallel / (1 + 1/(a+b))

  *** THEOREM 5b: Rapidity composition is a 'deformed parallel resistance': ***
    c = 1/(1/a + 1/b + 1/(ab))

  PROOF: 1/a + 1/b + 1/(ab) = (b + a + 1)/(ab) = (a+b+1)/(ab)
  So 1/(1/a + 1/b + 1/(ab)) = ab/(a+b+1) = c. QED!

  Verification of c = 1/(1/a + 1/b + 1/(ab)):
    (2,3): 1/(1/2+1/3+1/6) = 1 = 1, c = 1: True
    (4,5): 1/(1/4+1/5+1/20) = 2 = 2, c = 2: True
    (5,9): 1/(1/5+1/9+1/45) = 3 = 3, c = 3: True
    (6,7): 1/(1/6+1/7+1/42) = 3 = 3, c = 3: True
    (8,9): 1/(1/8+1/9+1/72) = 4 = 4, c = 4: True
    (3,8): 1/(1/3+1/8+1/24) = 2 = 2, c = 2: True

  This is BEAUTIFUL: rapidity composition = generalized parallel resistance
  with an extra '1/(ab)' term that breaks the standard parallel formula.

  EVEN CLEANER: 1/c + 1 = (1 + 1/a)(1 + 1/b)
  PROOF: 1/c + 1 = (a+b+1)/(ab) + 1 = (a+b+1+ab)/(ab) = (a+1)(b+1)/(ab).
         (1+1/a)(1+1/b) = (a+1)(b+1)/(ab). QED!

  *** THEOREM 5c (Multiplicative Structure): ***
    1 + 1/c = (1 + 1/a)(1 + 1/b)
  So the map a -> 1 + 1/a = (a+1)/a is a HOMOMORPHISM
  from rapidity composition to multiplication!

  Verification:
    c=1 from (2,3): 1+1/1 = 2 vs (1+1/2)(1+1/3) = 2: MATCH
    c=2 from (3,8): 1+1/2 = 3/2 vs (1+1/3)(1+1/8) = 3/2: MATCH
    c=2 from (4,5): 1+1/2 = 3/2 vs (1+1/4)(1+1/5) = 3/2: MATCH
    c=3 from (5,9): 1+1/3 = 4/3 vs (1+1/5)(1+1/9) = 4/3: MATCH
    c=3 from (6,7): 1+1/3 = 4/3 vs (1+1/6)(1+1/7) = 4/3: MATCH

  THIS IS THE KEY THEOREM. It says:
    (c+1)/c = ((a+1)/a) * ((b+1)/b)

  So define f(n) = (n+1)/n. Then f(a(+)b) = f(a)*f(b).
  f is a HOMOMORPHISM from (N, (+)) to (Q_{>1}, *).

  The map f(n) = (n+1)/n = 1 + 1/n sends:
    f(1) = 2, f(2) = 3/2, f(3) = 4/3, f(4) = 5/4, ...
  These are the SUPERPARTICULAR RATIOS = the musical intervals!

  So rapidity composition of music intervals IS multiplication
  of superparticular ratios, via the map n -> (n+1)/n.

  n-FOLD: f(a1 (+) a2 (+) ... (+) an) = f(a1)*f(a2)*...*f(an)
         = prod (ai+1)/ai

  For the triple abc/(a+1)(b+1)(d+1) - abd:
  1 + 1/e = (1+1/a)(1+1/b)(1+1/d) where e = a(+)b(+)d
  So (e+1)/e = (a+1)(b+1)(d+1)/(abd)
  e = abd / ((a+1)(b+1)(d+1) - abd)

  This CONFIRMS Theorem 1 via the homomorphism!

  Triple verification via homomorphism:
    (3,8,3)->e=1: f(e) = 2, f(a)f(b)f(d) = 2: True
    (4,5,3)->e=1: f(e) = 2, f(a)f(b)f(d) = 2: True
    (4,15,2)->e=1: f(e) = 2, f(a)f(b)f(d) = 2: True
    (4,15,8)->e=2: f(e) = 3/2, f(a)f(b)f(d) = 3/2: True
    (5,9,2)->e=1: f(e) = 2, f(a)f(b)f(d) = 2: True

  THE HOMOMORPHISM f(n) = (n+1)/n UNIFIES EVERYTHING:
  - Pair composition: immediate from f(c) = f(a)*f(b)
  - Triple composition: f(e) = f(a)*f(b)*f(d)
  - Integrality: e is a positive integer iff
    prod(a_i+1)/prod(a_i) = (e+1)/e with e integer
    iff prod(a_i) | (prod(a_i+1) - prod(a_i)) * something

  CONNECTION TO PRONIC NUMBERS:
  For pair: f(c) = f(a)*f(b) means (c+1)/c = (a+1)(b+1)/(ab)
  c(c+1) = ab * (c(c+1)/c(c+1))... Let's work directly:
  (a-c)(b-c) = c(c+1) follows from c = ab/(a+b+1).
  Via homomorphism: c = ab/(a+b+1) iff 1+1/c = (1+1/a)(1+1/b).
  These are EQUIVALENT characterizations.


========================================================================
BONUS: COTANGENT PRODUCT AND RAPIDITY ALGEBRA UNIFICATION
========================================================================

  The cotangent product identity (Theorem 2b) gives:
    prod_{k=1}^{n-1} cot(pi*k/n) = (-1)^{(n-1)/2}/n

  In the Cayley transform picture, cot(theta/2) = Q(e^{i*theta})/i.
  So the product of Cayley-transformed polygon vertices
  is related to 1/n.

  For the n-gon: Q maps vertices to i*cot(pi*k/n).
  Product of |Q(omega_k)| for k=1..n-1:
    = prod |cot(pi*k/n)| = 1/n

  POLYGON-RAPIDITY BRIDGE:
  The regular (2n+1)-gon has cot product 1/(2n+1).
  The n-th musical interval IS 1/(2n+1) in velocity space.
  So the (2n+1)-gon 'encodes' the n-th interval via its cotangent product!

  For the pair composition c = ab/(a+b+1):
  The (2c+1)-gon has cot product 1/(2c+1)
  = 1/((2a+1)(2b+1)/(2a+1 + 2b+1 - 1))
  Hmm, not quite. But the homomorphism connection IS:
    1/(2c+1) = velocity_c = v_a (+) v_b in rapidity space
  while the (2c+1)-gon has prod cot = 1/(2c+1) = velocity_c.

  *** OBSERVATION: The cotangent product of the (2n+1)-gon
  equals the rapidity velocity of the n-th musical interval. ***


========================================================================
SUMMARY OF NEW THEOREMS
========================================================================

  THM 1: Triple composition a(+)b(+)d = abd/((a+1)(b+1)(d+1) - abd)
         Generalizes to n-fold via the homomorphism.

  THM 2: prod_{k=1}^{n-1} cot(pi*k/n) = (-1)^{(n-1)/2}/n for ALL odd n
         PROOF via cyclotomic evaluation at x=1 and x=-1.

  THM 3: prod_{p<=N} (p+1)/(p-1) is integer only for N in {2,3,5,7,19}
         No further integers up to p=10000. Values: 3,6,9,12,21.

  THM 4: Cayley orbit of 1/phi is {1/phi, phi^3, -phi, -1/phi^3}.
         Q^k(F_n/F_{n+1}) traces a 4-cycle in Fibonacci index space.
         Q on general F_n/F_m rarely stays in Fibonacci ratios.

  THM 5 (KEY): The map f(n) = (n+1)/n is a HOMOMORPHISM from
         rapidity composition to multiplication:
           f(a (+) b) = f(a) * f(b)
           1 + 1/c = (1 + 1/a)(1 + 1/b)
         This is the deepest structural theorem, unifying:
         - The pronic factorization (a-c)(b-c) = c(c+1)
         - The triple composition formula
         - The n-fold generalization
         - The resistance-parallel analogy c = 1/(1/a + 1/b + 1/(ab))
         Rapidity composition IS superparticular ratio multiplication!

  COROLLARY: The number of n-fold compositions to c equals the number of
  ways to write (c+1)/c as a product of n superparticular ratios (k+1)/k.

